{"id":"paid-above-the-scale","title":"Paid far above the top of the published scale, year after year","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Which employees are paid regular salary well above the highest published rate for the job class the payroll puts them in, in more than one year?","finding":"70 employees were paid regular salary at least a quarter above the highest published rate for their job class, and a quarter above their classmates, in two or more fiscal years since 2017; 43 of them in five years or more and 40 still in fiscal 2026. The pay above the top of the scale adds up to $27.2 million. 61 of the 70 are in the file’s engineering job families, which include the architecture classes, and the departments are Public Utilities Commission (25), Airport Commission (21), Public Works (14), Municipal Transportation Agency (4). 19 of the 40 still above the line in fiscal 2026 were paid one of 6 identical salaries (the commonest, $286,278, went to 8 people), which points to a second pay schedule that the payroll file does not name. A further 117 employees were above the line in a single year only.","how":"City payroll file, fiscal 2017 to 2026 (the years with hours), against both pay tables published by the Department of Human Resources. For each employee, class and year, regular salary is divided by hours paid (at least 1,040, half a year); overtime and “other” pay are left out, and because the hours include overtime hours the rate is if anything understated. That rate is compared with the highest step or range ceiling published for the class at any date up to the end of the fiscal year, including the extended steps that need special approval. An employee is listed when the rate is more than 25% above that ceiling and, where the class has five or more holders, more than 25% above the middle rate actually paid in the class, in at least two years. The “top of scale” column is the ceiling for the same number of hours. 314,765 employee-years could be compared; 7,839 were in classes with no published rate. Ranked by total pay above the ceiling.","limits":"A likely explanation is that the person is doing a higher-level job than the class shown, for example an acting assignment or a second appointment to a higher post held alongside permanent status in a lower class, with the payroll file reporting the permanent class. That would be an ordinary arrangement, and it would explain why the same amounts recur; the payroll alone cannot confirm it. The few department heads on the list are a different case: the union column marks some senior transportation posts as individual employment contracts. A single high year can be back pay or a settlement, which is why two years are required. What the payroll cannot show is which post each person actually fills and who approved the rate; the appointment form and the position record would. The pay tables on disk begin on 1 July 2014 for stepped classes and on 8 January 2022 for pay ranges; a rate published only elsewhere would not be seen.","columns":[{"k":"person","t":"Name on the payroll","type":"person"},{"k":"job","t":"Job class shown","type":"text"},{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"years","t":"Years above the line","type":"int"},{"k":"span","t":"Between","type":"text"},{"k":"salary","t":"Regular salary, latest such year","type":"usd"},{"k":"scaleTop","t":"Top of the published scale for the same hours","type":"usd"},{"k":"over","t":"Above the top","type":"pct"},{"k":"excess","t":"Paid above the top, all years","type":"usd"},{"k":"type","t":"Type of appointment","type":"text"}],"rows":[{"person":"Richard Thall Jr","job":"Landscape Architect Assoc 2 (class 5272)","department":"Airport Commission","years":10,"span":"Fiscal 2017 to 2026","salary":311446,"scaleTop":191511,"over":0.626,"excess":901644,"type":"Permanent Civil Service"},{"person":"Joe Chin","job":"Engineer (class 5241)","department":"Public Works","years":10,"span":"Fiscal 2017 to 2026","salary":319181,"scaleTop":217566,"over":0.467,"excess":797304,"type":"Permanent Civil Service"},{"person":"Edmund Lee","job":"Junior Engineer (class 5201)","department":"Public Works","years":10,"span":"Fiscal 2017 to 2026","salary":286278,"scaleTop":142965,"over":1.002,"excess":784619,"type":"Permanent Civil Service"},{"person":"Jignesh Desai","job":"Engineer (class 5241)","department":"Public Utilities Commission","years":10,"span":"Fiscal 2017 to 2026","salary":311446,"scaleTop":217566,"over":0.432,"excess":754567,"type":"Permanent Civil Service"},{"person":"Christine Tang","job":"Assoc Engineer (class 5207)","department":"Public Works","years":10,"span":"Fiscal 2017 to 2026","salary":286278,"scaleTop":191511,"over":0.495,"excess":753595,"type":"Permanent Civil Service"},{"person":"Susan Hou","job":"Engineer (class 5241)","department":"Public Utilities Commission","years":10,"span":"Fiscal 2017 to 2026","salary":319180,"scaleTop":217566,"over":0.467,"excess":728257,"type":"Permanent Civil Service"},{"person":"Kristin Opbroek","job":"Landscape Architect Assoc 2 (class 5272)","department":"Airport Commission","years":10,"span":"Fiscal 2017 to 2026","salary":293419,"scaleTop":191511,"over":0.532,"excess":727265,"type":"Permanent Civil Service"},{"person":"Bryan Dessaure","job":"Assoc Engineer (class 5207)","department":"Public Utilities Commission","years":10,"span":"Fiscal 2017 to 2026","salary":286278,"scaleTop":191511,"over":0.495,"excess":715480,"type":"Permanent Civil Service"},{"person":"Claudia Luquin","job":"Architectural Associate 1 (class 5265)","department":"Airport Commission","years":6,"span":"Fiscal 2017 to 2022","salary":249990,"scaleTop":125918,"over":0.985,"excess":695960,"type":"Permanent Civil Service"},{"person":"Ramon Kong","job":"Engineer (class 5241)","department":"Public Works","years":10,"span":"Fiscal 2017 to 2026","salary":319180,"scaleTop":217566,"over":0.467,"excess":684558,"type":"Permanent Civil Service"},{"person":"Paul Barradas","job":"Assistant Engineer (class 52